Quickstart Prerequisites JDK 11+ (Scala 3 projects need a recent JDK anyway) deder build tool (this project is built with it) Get the code git clone https://github.com/sake92/codeps.git
cd codeps
 Generate analysis input codeps reads what your compiler already produced — no build integration required. Scala — compile with SemanticDB enabled (here with scala-cli): scala-cli compile --server=false --semanticdb -d classes src/
# -> classes/META-INF/semanticdb/**/*.semanticdb
 Java or mixed — use the JDK's own analyzer: jdeps -verbose:package -filter:none -cp classes classes > jdeps.txt
 See SemanticDB analysis and jdeps analysis for details. Analyze Run the CLI with deder: # SemanticDB input: point at the directory containing *.semanticdb files
deder exec -t run -m cli semdb classes/META-INF/semanticdb -i com.example -f dot

# jdeps input: point at the jdeps output file
deder exec -t run -m cli jdeps jdeps.txt -i com.example -f dot
 Both print the dependency graph to stdout. Example output: digraph deps {
  "com.example.app" -> "com.example.modules.module2";
  "com.example.modules.module1" -> "com.example.util";
  "com.example.modules.module2" -> "com.example.modules.module1";
}
 Visualize Export as JSON and open it in the interactive demo: deder exec -t run -m cli semdb classes/META-INF/semanticdb -i com.example -f json -o graph.json
 Then open the demo page and drop graph.json onto it (or use Paste / Load). What's next? Filter and collapse packages: CLI reference Export to other formats: Exporting graphs Understand the internals: Architecture

Exporting graphs Every codeps run ends with one of three export formats, selected with -f/--format: Format Use case dot Graphviz rendering, static images, dot -Tsvg pipelines json Programmatic consumption, the interactive demo mermaid Markdown documentation, Mermaid live editors, GitHub markdown Output goes to stdout by default; use -o/--out to write to a file. DOT deder exec -t run -m cli semdb classes/META-INF/semanticdb -i com.example -f dot -o graph.dot
dot -Tsvg graph.dot -o graph.svg   # render with graphviz
 digraph deps {
  "com.example.app" -> "com.example.modules.module2";
  "com.example.modules.module1" -> "com.example.util";
  "com.example.modules.module2" -> "com.example.modules.module1";
}
 Nodes that have no edges are emitted as standalone lines, so they are not lost. JSON deder exec -t run -m cli semdb classes/META-INF/semanticdb -i com.example -f json -o graph.json
 {
  "nodes": ["com.example.app", "com.example.modules.module1", "com.example.modules.module2", "com.example.util"],
  "edges": [["com.example.app", "com.example.modules.module2"], ["com.example.modules.module1", "com.example.util"], ["com.example.modules.module2", "com.example.modules.module1"]],
  "nodeInfo": {
    "com.example.app": {"files": 1, "classes": 1},
    "com.example.modules.module1": {"files": 1, "classes": 1},
    "com.example.modules.module2": {"files": 1, "classes": 1},
    "com.example.util": {"files": 1, "classes": 1}
  }
}
 nodes — package names (sorted) edges — pairs [source, target] (sorted) nodeInfo — per-package {files, classes} stats; only present for SemanticDB input (jdeps has no stats) This is the format the interactive demo consumes — export, then drop the file on the page. Mermaid deder exec -t run -m cli semdb classes/META-INF/semanticdb -i com.example -f mermaid
 flowchart LR
  N0["com.example.app"]
  N1["com.example.modules.module1"]
  N2["com.example.modules.module2"]
  N3["com.example.util"]
  N0 --> N2
  N1 --> N3
  N2 --> N1
 Node ids are aliased to N0, N1, ... because Mermaid ids break on dots. Paste the output into any Mermaid renderer (e.g. mermaid.live) or a Markdown file: ```mermaid
flowchart LR
  N0["com.example.app"] --> N2["com.example.modules.module2"]
```
 See Export formats for format details.

SemanticDB analysis SemanticDB is a data format describing the semantic information of Scala (and Java) programs, produced by the Scala compiler (-Xsemanticdb flag) or tools like scala-cli. The semdb subcommand walks a directory, reads every *.semanticdb file, and extracts: own packages — packages defined in the analyzed sources package edges — for every symbol reference, an edge own package -> referenced package stats — per package: number of source files and number of class-like types (classes, objects, traits) Generating SemanticDB files With scala-cli: scala-cli compile --server=false --semanticdb -d classes src/
 This writes one .semanticdb file per source file under classes/META-INF/semanticdb/. Other ways to get SemanticDB output: scalac directly: add -Xsemanticdb (and optionally -P:semanticdb:sourceroot:...) to your compile flags Maven / sbt: enable the semanticdb compiler plugin and check the generated files in the target dir The codeps repo itself ships a checked-in example: testFixtures/example1 is compiled by tests into tmp/examples/example1/classes/META-INF/semanticdb Running the analysis deder exec -t run -m cli semdb <dir-with-semanticdb> -i com.example -f dot
 <dir-with-semanticdb> is a positional argument — the whole directory tree is walked for .semanticdb files -i/--include keeps only your packages (see filtering) -f/--format selects the output format: dot, json or mermaid Example, using the fixture in this repo: deder exec -t run -m cli semdb tmp/examples/example1/classes/META-INF/semanticdb -i com.example -f dot
 digraph deps {
  "com.example.app" -> "com.example.modules.module2";
  "com.example.modules.module1" -> "com.example.util";
  "com.example.modules.module2" -> "com.example.modules.module1";
}
 The JSON format also includes per-package stats (nodeInfo), which the interactive demo can display on nodes (file/class counts): {
  "nodes": ["com.example.app", "com.example.modules.module1"],
  "edges": [["com.example.app", "com.example.modules.module2"]],
  "nodeInfo": {
    "com.example.app": {"files": 1, "classes": 1}
  }
}
 Filtering --include and --exclude take package patterns: a pattern com.example matches the package itself and everything below it. Excludes win over includes. # only com.example packages, no third-party or JDK noise
deder exec -t run -m cli semdb classes/META-INF/semanticdb -i com.example -f json

# com.example.* minus internal helpers
deder exec -t run -m cli semdb classes/META-INF/semanticdb -i com.example -e com.example.internal -f json
 Collapsing Collapse rules merge whole subtrees into a single node, which keeps big graphs readable: Pattern Effect com.example.** everything below com.example collapses into com.example org.lib.* sub-packages collapse one level below the prefix (e.g. org.lib.http, org.lib.json) deder exec -t run -m cli semdb classes/META-INF/semanticdb -i com.example -c com.example.modules.** -f dot
 When multiple rules match, the longest prefix wins; loops created by collapsing are dropped.

jdeps analysis jdeps is the JDK's own dependency analyzer. It ships with every JDK, so this workflow needs no extra tooling — great for Java projects or when you can't (or don't want to) enable SemanticDB. The jdeps subcommand parses jdeps -verbose:package text output and extracts package-to-package edges. Note that jdeps carries no per-package file/class info, so the JSON nodeInfo stats are omitted for this input. Generating the input jdeps -verbose:package -filter:none -cp classes classes > jdeps.txt
 -verbose:package is required — codeps ignores summary lines and reads the indented detail lines -filter:none keeps all edges (otherwise JDK-internal noise like java.* is dropped; you can filter it later with --exclude) The codeps repo ships a checked-in example: tmp/examples/example1/jdeps.txt (generated by tests from the testFixtures/example1 classes). Running the analysis deder exec -t run -m cli jdeps jdeps.txt -i com.example -f dot
 Example, using the fixture in this repo: deder exec -t run -m cli jdeps tmp/examples/example1/jdeps.txt -i com.example -f dot
 digraph deps {
  "com.example.app" -> "com.example.modules.module2";
  "com.example.modules.module1" -> "com.example.util";
  "com.example.modules.module2" -> "com.example.modules.module1";
}
 Filtering JDK noise The raw jdeps -verbose:package output includes edges to java.*, scala.* and other platform packages. Exclude them to keep the graph focused on your code: deder exec -t run -m cli jdeps jdeps.txt -i com.example -e java.** -e scala.** -f json
 Note: excludes are package patterns matched against the source package of each edge — java.** collapses everything under java (see Collapse rules). Collapsing Just like with SemanticDB input, -c/--collapse rules apply: deder exec -t run -m cli jdeps jdeps.txt -i com.example -c com.example.modules.** -f dot
 See CLI reference for the full option list.

Export formats All exporters receive the final graph: nodes = packages after filtering and collapsing, edges = package dependencies (self-edges removed, deduplicated). DOT Rendered by DotExporter. Graphviz digraph with quoted node ids: digraph deps {
  "com.example.app" -> "com.example.modules.module2";
  "com.example.modules.module1" -> "com.example.util";
}
 edges are sorted by (source, target) isolated vertices (no edges) are emitted as standalone lines quotes and backslashes inside package names are escaped Render it with Graphviz: deder exec -t run -m cli semdb classes/META-INF/semanticdb -i com.example -f dot -o graph.dot
dot -Tsvg graph.dot -o graph.svg
 JSON Rendered by JsonExporter. Shape: {
  "nodes": ["com.example.app", "com.example.modules.module1"],
  "edges": [["com.example.app", "com.example.modules.module2"]],
  "nodeInfo": {
    "com.example.app": {"files": 1, "classes": 1}
  }
}
 nodes — sorted package names edges — [source, target] pairs, sorted nodeInfo — per-package {files, classes} stats; present only for SemanticDB input (jdeps output carries no file/class info), and only for nodes with known stats This is the input format of the interactive demo. Mermaid Rendered by MermaidExporter. flowchart LR with aliased node ids (N0, N1, ...) because Mermaid ids break on dots: flowchart LR
  N0["com.example.app"]
  N1["com.example.modules.module1"]
  N0 --> N1
 Nodes are listed first (sorted, each with its N<i> alias and quoted label), then edges as N<i> --> N<j> (sorted by source/target). Paste into any Mermaid renderer, or embed in Markdown: ```mermaid
flowchart LR
  N0["com.example.app"] --> N1["com.example.modules.module1"]
```

codeps codeps is a code package dependency analyzer for JVM projects (Java and Scala). It parses existing compiler output — SemanticDB or jdeps — and produces a package-level dependency graph, which you can export as DOT, JSON or Mermaid. No build-system integration needed: it just reads what your compiler already produced. New here? Start with the Quickstart. Want to explore a graph interactively? Try the interactive demo — it loads graphs exported with -f json. Features Two input formats: SemanticDB — detailed, from Scala compiler output (.semanticdb files), with per-package file/class stats jdeps — from the JDK's own jdeps -verbose:package output, no extra tooling needed Filtering — keep only packages matching --include patterns, drop noise with --exclude (e.g. java.*, scala.*) Collapsing — merge whole package subtrees with --collapse rules (com.example.**, org.lib.*) Three export formats — DOT, JSON, Mermaid Interactive demo — a standalone graph viewer: layouts, filtering, degree analysis, package suggestions Quick example deder exec -t run -m cli semdb tmp/examples/example1/classes/META-INF/semanticdb -i com.example -f dot
 digraph deps {
  "com.example.app" -> "com.example.modules.module2";
  "com.example.modules.module1" -> "com.example.util";
  "com.example.modules.module2" -> "com.example.modules.module1";
}
